WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.027
Null values require special care in SQL comparisons.

00:00:04.027 --> 00:00:11.852
If you compare a value with null using the normal equals operator, the result is not simply true or false.

00:00:11.852 --> 00:00:15.582
SQL treats the comparison as unknown.

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:21.063
That behavior can make update conditions difficult when nullable columns are involved.

00:00:21.671 --> 00:00:24.893
Postgres provides the phrase is distinct from.

00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:29.773
It gives us a null-safe way to ask whether two values are different.

00:00:29.773 --> 00:00:35.313
For example, suppose we compare an existing email with a cleaned version.

00:00:35.313 --> 00:00:41.953
We want to know whether an update would actually change the value, even if one side could be null.

00:00:41.953 --> 00:00:47.598
Using is distinct from makes that comparison explicit and predictable.

00:00:47.598 --> 00:00:54.289
You do not need to use it everywhere, but it is a very useful Postgres feature when cleaning nullable data.
